  <h1>City of Wonders</h1>
  <div class="story-meta">March 2026</div>
 </div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>Inside, the archives were intact. Rows of storage devices, each one labeled in the formal dialect with a date and a subject heading. Yuen recognized the format. She had grown up with records like these — her mother had worked in municipal administration before the restructuring, and Yuen remembered the feel of the labels, the weight of the cases, the particular smell of the older ones, which were paper and faintly sweet with age.</p>
<p>The building had no power. That was expected. But the storage devices were not electronic, or not only electronic — they had a secondary layer, a physical component, as if the people who built this place had understood that information survives best when it is kept in more than one form. Reyes handled the first one gently, turning it in her hands, looking for a way to open it.</p>
<p>It opened in her palm. Not with a click or a mechanism, but with a kind of willingness — as if it had been waiting to be asked.</p>
<p>Inside was a single sheet of material, thin and smooth and the color of old bone. On it, in handwriting that looked printed rather than written, were two paragraphs. The first described the construction of the tower. The second described something else.</p>
<p><em>We did not build the tower to reach the sky,</em> it read. <em>We built it to reach each other. The sky was simply the direction that best showed us what we were doing. When the city fell — and we knew it would fall, we had seen the projections — we made a choice. We could abandon the project, seal the archives, let the city return to silence. Or we could keep the question alive.</em></p>
<p><em>We kept the question alive.</em></p>
<p><em>If you are reading this, you are part of the answer.</em></p>
<p>Yuen read it three times. She read it again after the team had gone to sleep, alone in the archive room, sitting on the floor with the storage device in her lap like a letter that had been sent a long time ago and had finally found its recipient.</p>
<p>She understood, then, what the figures were.</p>
<p>They were not people who had been preserved. They were not ghosts, not echoes, not copies. They were the question, made visible. They were what a city looks like when it is trying to remember what a city is for.</p>
<p>A city is not its buildings. A city is not its population density or its transit routes or its utility infrastructure. A city is a conversation — a sustained, collective act of attention in which strangers agree to pay attention to each other, to build structures that outlast the builder, to maintain a shared space that does not yet exist and must be continually created.</p>
<p>The people of the City of Wonders had understood this. When they fell, they had encoded this understanding into the city itself — into the pristine materials, the taller tower, the figures standing in doorways, the device on the observation deck, the archives seven floors below ground. They had made the city into a thought.</p>
<p>And thoughts, if they are rigorous enough, do not die. They wait.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>Solano died four years after the survey. A transport accident, unrelated to the city. Yuen learned about it from a notice in the weekly digest, three months after it happened. She did not know he had written anything in his personal log until years later, when the log was released to the archive as part of a larger collection and someone found the entry about the question.</p>
<p>By then, Yuen had been back to the city twice more. The second time, she brought a single colleague — a structural engineer named Paulo, who had asked to come after reading the official report and finding it incomplete in a way he couldn't name. They spent eight days in the city. On the fifth day, Paulo stood in the central plaza and said, without preamble: "They're not waiting for us to find them. They're waiting for us to remember them."</p>
<p>He meant the original inhabitants. But Yuen thought he meant something else, too.</p>
<p>The figures in the doorways — the 4,217 of them, standing in their white linen and gray wool with their open, focused, gone-empty eyes — were not waiting for the survey team. They were not waiting for Yuen to return, or for Paulo to arrive, or for any specific person to come and read the inscription and understand. They were waiting for the understanding itself. They were the shape that attention takes when it is sustained past the point where the person doing the attention has ceased to exist.</p>
<p>The city's original inhabitants had not lived forever. But they had been very, very careful about what they meant when they said <em>we</em>. And they had found a way to make that meaning persist.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>On Yuen's third visit, she brought nothing. No scanner, no camera, no equipment of any kind. She left Paulo at the transport and walked into the city alone, the way she had driven in alone on that first day, and she walked until she reached the tower.</p>
<p>She climbed it the same way she had the first time — on foot, through the interior stairwell that spiraled up through the structure's center. She had counted the steps on her first climb. Four thousand two hundred and sixteen. She counted them again this time, though she already knew the number, because counting was a way of paying attention, and she wanted to pay attention now in a way she had not managed before.</p>
<p>At the top, the device waited. She stood in front of it. She read the inscription — not for the first time, but for the first time without expectation. Without the need to understand, or to report, or to figure out what it meant so she could explain it to someone else.</p>
<p>She read it the way Solano had read it. She sat down on the floor.</p>
<p><em>WE ARE STILL HERE. WE ARE WAITING FOR YOU TO CATCH UP.</em></p>
<p>She stayed until the light changed, until the sun moved low enough that the device's surface caught the color of the evening, until the letters were outlined in gold instead of white. She stayed until the silence in the room had a texture she could feel against her skin.</p>
<p>Then she said: "I know what you are."</p>
<p>She did not expect an answer. She was not sure an answer was possible, or whether possible was even the right frame. The city was not a machine. It was not an intelligence in the way she understood intelligence. But it had been paying attention for three hundred years, and she understood that some forms of attention do not require a response to be complete.</p>
<p>The question did not need her to answer it. The question needed her to become an answer.</p>
<p>She was not sure what that meant. She was not sure it meant anything she could translate into words. But she stayed there, on the floor of the observation deck, in the changing light, and she let the question hold her the way the question had held the figures in the doorways — not as a person being preserved, but as a thought being held.</p>
<p>When she descended the tower, it was dark. The figures were still in their doorways, still on their corners, still standing in their postures of waiting. She walked among them on her way back to the transport. She did not feel watched. She felt witnessed.</p>
<p>There is a difference, and she had learned it.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>The official record of Yuen's third visit was a single page, filed at headquarters, noting that she had traveled alone to the city and returned without incident. No new data was recorded. No samples were collected. No measurements were taken.</p>
<p>In her personal log — now paper, like Solano's had been, because she had come to understand that some things survive best in more than one form — she wrote:</p>
<p><em>The city is not a place we go to.</em></p>
<p><em>The city is a place we go back to.</em></p>
<p><em>Not because it remembers us.</em></p>
<p><em>Because we are the part of it that remembers.</em></p>
<p>She kept the log in a drawer in her quarters. She did not share it. She did not need to. The city's original inhabitants had not written their understanding on the walls of the tower or in the archives or on the surface of the device. They had written it in the structure of the city itself — in the pristine materials, in the taller tower, in the figures who stood in doorways because doorways are where conversations happen, where inside meets outside, where one person acknowledges another.</p>
